have returned  path capability with set-top box data  unavailable and then all these other  devices that people are increasingly  using plus of course all those homes  that  are not covered by set-top box data  because maybe the data isn't available  or maybe that just for digital return  path capability isn't there  it's a lot of challenges now the concept  of total survey error is one that we  always have to be mindful of i think  when we're looking at integrating these  data because we've got  with set top box for potential for much  reduced standard error because we've got  much larger samples and that's really  the benefit but because of all the  issues i've mentioned we also have that  the problem of bias  with our people meter samples it's the  other way around we have potentially  large standard error because the samples  are not as big as we would like them to  be because of economic factors  but the bias is acknowledged to be very  low because we've got a complete  measurement so putting those two things  together should give us the best of both  worlds but this concept of total survey  era is i think a fundamental one for us  to determine whether we're improving the  measurement  so some examples of set-top box data  quality on the left what we've got is  you can see those little spikes and  those are spikes about two percent of  the population changing channels at  program junctions and that's  fairly sort of standard what we'd expect  to see based on what we know about tv  viewing so that's fine but on the right  you can see a big spike there at night  and it wasn't an earthquake it wasn't  like the data we just saw before this is  a machine reboot activity where the the  cable provider is downloading  information um  and it looks like something's happening  on the set-top box in terms of somebody  viewing it changing channel and so on  but of course  nothing like that is happening but  it's just a machine generated activity  so just an example of the sorts of  things we we have to address  so at the end of the day and you know as  i was saying about total survey era are  we improving measurement well  we have to be very transparent and we  have to validate each step and overall  and we have to have some sort of  statistical rigor and what we're seeing  here is a comparison of  overall ratings for women 18 plus for  monday to friday in november 2010  and pretty stable when we're comparing  our people meter the blue line with our  hybridized measurement but you can see  there is a bit of a gap there but i've  highlighted  and what we found with that is that it  was not statistically significant it was  close to being but it wasn't quite  statistically significant but what we're  doing with this is we're assessing for  each pair of data points whether we do  have statistical significance and then  looking about overall distribution to  see whether what we've got with our  hybrid measurement appears to  be representative of the population  and again the total survey era concept  here for females 18 plus we've got a  reduction in our total survey era as we  move to hybrid of 19  for females 18 to 34  38 and this is where we you see more  gains with the smaller demographics  because the sample sizes are smaller  there  so clearly  there's benefit here to doing this and  we've demonstrated it through a sort of  statistical process  so that's it on set top box i now want  to  talk about broader issues of integration  errors  and various things to to discuss input  error matching error and so on and i'll  get straight onto them so input error  garbage in garbage out not much you can  do about that um if the data isn't there  or if it's wrong or it doesn't quite  give you what you want then data  integration doesn't normally solve that  problem  other than you can sometimes get  improvements through integration  weighting effects an example may be that  you have a data set that is not very  good in terms of representing  young adults  because it's difficult to recruit to a  survey for example  so when you look at your overall  measurement of say 18 plus individuals  that affects the results now clearly you  can solve that through a weighting  technique just on its own but you can  also solve that almost immediately  through integration if you're then  fusing that data set onto a  representative sample and this is  something we see quite a lot with our  data fusion business where we're taking  our television panel data and we are  fusing information onto it our  television panel is a very good solid  representative sample of the population  in the u.s and by fusing data onto it  we're changing that fuse data and making  it more representative  now clearly there's only so far you can  go with that if you've got a really  really bad sample and you don't have if  you have a tiny percentage of say young  hispanics and you're interested in young  hispanics you're not going to solve  problems just through that sort of  waiting but  you know  in most cases you find you're in a  reasonably good position to um to  mitigate errors i think it's an  important point because i think a lot of  people say well  um if you know the data is what it is  and it doesn't get any better through  integration well it can do because it  can  you can approach it in a slightly more  rigorous  fashion now i'm not talking about  matching error for address matching  because this is increasingly an area  that  we're getting involved with and  it just seems a whole industry the whole  media industry and related advertisers  are getting involved with taking very  large databases maybe customer  relationship databases and then matching  as much as they can on a name and  address level and we've done quite a lot  of that  in various parts of our business around  advertising effectiveness  and  what we find is that from a marketing  perspective this works really well  because you just go to a client say  we've got single source data but it  isn't really single source data it's  address match data and you don't always  get a perfect match and i like to think  about sort of the good the bad and the  ugly good obviously is when you get a  correct match the bad is well you didn't  get a match tool but the ugly can be  when you get an incorrect match when you  think you know something about a home or  an individual but actually you don't  because you've incorrectly matched  excuse me and what you get there though  is a trade-off between match rates and  error rates because it's also not  necessarily a simple equation of this is  the exact address and the exact name  there's maybe some  slight difference then you have to make  a decision whether it's sort of close  enough is it a sort of 90 match and will  you accept that in doing that you're  going to get more matches but you  introduce the possibility of incorrect  matches  one thing i would say though is that you  if you go into multiple database  integrations  i don't think that the the error rate so  you say you had a 90 match rate on one  match and you get another 90 on another  it doesn't necessarily mean to say that  you you've  you've got a sort of 80 match overall  what you may find is you've actually  missed 10 of the population who aren't  covered on either of those databases so  you just have to be sort of conscious of  what you've got and  understand the databases and their  limitations it's difficult to to be very  sort of  you know to definitely say that there is  a multiplicative effect  so that's matching error on  names and addresses  a lot of what we're doing though is more  statistical imputation where we're  matching on a set of common  characteristics  that maybe say demographic or media  based  or purchase based  information  and in doing that then you can  introduce model bias leading to what  some people call attenuation others call  regression to the mean  um but when you're looking at any  individual data point that bias that the  model has can be undetectable just  because you've got sampling error and so  there's a level of noise around that and  this  chart sort of gives an example of that  what we've got here  is a thousand product category users  analyzed in terms of their total hours  of weekly television viewing and i've  sorted it  by  the truth the actual data  and then we've done a fusion test to see  how good the fusion is at predicting uh  those hours of viewing  so the pink line is the actual  data going from the lowest product uh  use of about 20 hours per week to the  highest one right about 35 hours a week  and then you can see the blue fused data  and it's bouncing up and down and that's  because we've got samples and we know  we're not going to get it exactly right  what we're trying to do is estimate what  the population does  and there's clearly some sampling air  around that but what this shows is that  fusion works pretty well it does track  the the pink line pretty well if we put  a best fit through which is the yellow  line you can see that  it's pretty good all the way through  apart from right at the left hand side  and right at the right hand side and  that could be some evidence of  regression to the mean there where  the  the lightest viewing is not quite as  light in in the fuse data and the  heaviest viewing is not quite as heavy  so you've got a little bit of  attenuation going on there  but as i say any look at any single any  one of those thousand points and you  wouldn't necessarily conclude that that  it was biased but it could just be by  chance variation  so how do we get around that how do we  estimate bias well what we do is for  each of those pairs of points we  calculate um a z-test and compare the  real and natural and then we look at the  distribution of z-scores convert those  two to probabilities of um other  currents and then compare them with  actual and what we've got here on the on  that pink straight line is the fact that  we would expect  that one  observation to be um difference at the  one in a thousand level and two of the  two in a thousand level and so on  because we've got a thousand  observations so we get a straight line  but what we've actually got is a  slightly um  broader  spread which is the s shape and that gap  that gap between the s shape and the  straight line that we can assume to be  the  bias that we've introduced into the  uh into the measurement so that's  typically how we do as i say it doesn't  tell you for any individual point but  you've got it wrong but it does give you  some overall feeling of the the error  that you've introduced  now when it comes to integrating  multiple data sets using statistical  techniques fusion imputation and so on  there are various ways that you can  approach it i've got two ways here the  hub and spoke approach and in a sort of  sequential approach and what we've got  here based on a single source data set  we have of our people meter television  data internet data and credit card  purchase data  is first of all on the left we're fusing  the the web use onto the tv panel and  then we're fusing the the buy purchase  data from credit card data separately  onto whereas on the right  we do the same thing for internet first  of all we just fuse the internet data  but then when we're doing the fusion of  the buy data we're also taking into  account internet usage that we have on  our buy data set  and using that so we're fusing both to  the tv and the internet data  simultaneously  when we do that in this test the  accuracy clearly improves and the  correlation between um internet usage  and  and purchase data improves from 0.4 to  point five and our overall correlation  between tv  um and internet um  and and buy goes up from point four four  to point six seven we're making best use  of the data here because we are taking  into account all the information that  we've got from the internet data  when we're we're fusing the buy data and  you can see that it's uh improving it  this is early days of this test we're  just working through this there's a lot  more we need to do but i think these are  indicative of the sorts of things that  you would expect to see that you really  want to try and use all the data that's  available to you when you're doing  fusion  fusions like this and and you do get  improvements  so  if the hub data in this case the  television data had all the relevant  linking information then um then it  wouldn't make any difference but in this  case because we're adding in the  internet data and then subsequently  using it in the sequential fusion we get  a better result  the problem you find with this though is  that it kind of works well for three  data sets but moment you start to move  beyond that then you need to be very  careful things turn into a bit of a like  mess because there are so many  intracorrelations that you need to be  concerned about and so  care needs to be taken i think when when  you're when you're doing something like  that  okay um just about running out of time  validity levels individual versus  aggregated um i won't dwell on this  because of time considerations too much  but a very important consideration here  is individual level uh predictability  versus aggregate level and a lot of what  we're doing works at the aggregate level  but  it doesn't work particularly well at the  individual level in in terms of a  perfect prediction of whether somebody  does or does not use a particular brand  of shampoo but what you can do is  predict  uh viewing levels of  that particular brands  users so that's the sort of difference  that said individual prediction even if  it's not  perfect for all uses it can still give  you improvements so  we do work with precision marketing  where we're getting 40 improvement on  random when predicting product users  based on cookie prediction so what does  that mean it means when an online  appetizer advertises they're reaching  say 14  of that product you of those product  users or 14 percent of the cookies  they're targeting um give you those  product users whereas they would have  had 10 if they hadn't done it so it's  worthwhile doing that and that can  lead them to  to win in bids for programmatic buying  by i say bidding 30 percent higher as an  arbitrage there where they're they're  getting 40 gain for 30 extra cost  okay so  in conclusion there does seem to be data  everywhere at the moment that's a great  problem to have  the data quality and relevance is  essential now as it was back in the  1930s it has its statistical integrity  and obviously you can get insights from  all this stuff but you can also get  error and  that's you know clearly something we all  have to be conscious of and  remember ralph landon  okay that's it thanks very much
 

